<article class="env-definition" id="0EUL">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0EUL">Definition <span data-tag="0EUL">29.48.1</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We say $A$ is <em>seminormal</em> if for all $x, y \in A$ with $x^3 = y^2$ there is a unique $a \in A$ with $x = a^2$ and $y = a^3$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say $A$ is <em>absolutely weakly normal</em> if (a) $A$ is seminormal and (b) for any prime number $p$ and $x, y \in A$ with $p^ px = y^ p$ there is a unique $a \in A$ with $x = a^ p$ and $y = pa$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>